    {
      "finding": "asm-002",
      "action": "Model and valuation now has three changes. (1) The net-cash bullet carries a labelled judgment: the customer-warrant liability is excluded from {{net_cash}} because it is equity-settled ([[Q-13]], not settleable in cash), and its economic cost is captured by the dilution sensitivity instead. (2) A new 'Customer warrants' bullet quotes [[Q-12]] (two warrants, each up to 160M shares at $0.01) and [[Q-16]] (none vested). It paraphrases Q-17 (contingently issuable, excluded from diluted EPS) and says the warrant shares are therefore excluded from the share count behind {{target_price}}. A labelled judgment gives direction and size in words, with no digits: full vesting would lower the target by roughly one-sixth. This is an upper bound, not an expected value; the analyst arithmetic is in valuation_inputs.json rationales.customer_warrants and is not a code output. Because dilution pushes the implied return further below the {{rating_underperform_max}} band, the rating would remain {{rating}}. (3) A new 'Warrant contra-revenue' bullet quotes [[Q-15]] ($4.5bn to reduce revenue). It states that future reported revenue will be reduced as qualifying sales occur, and that we treat this as non-cash without cutting forecast cash flow, to avoid double counting (judgment). Risks gets a one-line dilution-risk bullet pointing to the valuation section. Q-14, Q-17 (as a quote) and Q-18 are not used, to keep the note short."
    },
